Z||000001||Oreif||08-24-2000||11:44 AM||||There is no such thing as a "Formula GT"<BR>It sounds like a car ad. The person probably has an 86 GT and it looks longer to him with the fastback body style than the notchback style. The 2.8L engines are Chevy designed engines. So all V-6 fiero's have a "fact chevy eng". It is not that rare. What is the person asking for it? With an ad like that he probably want 3 times what it is really worth.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||08-24-2000||08:00 PM||||I wouldn't totally count out the posibility of a GT/Formula being made, but in '86?! I'd bet the guy is full of sh!t, or doesn't know what he's talking about. I don't know if GM's Central Office Production Order system was still in use in the '80s, but in the late '60s- early '70s, you could special-order a car with multiple overlaying options (remember the Camaro SS/RS). Basically, if the dealer was willing to participate, you could get the car built any way you want it. Still, I doubt a rare factory built GT/Formula exists.<P>Where did you see that ad? It sounds familiar.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-25-2000||06:33 AM||||rockcrawl--Although it is possible to get parts of multiple models, I don't think it's possible to have a GT/Formula.  The Formula was basically a stripped down GT notchback.  Or it was a base model coupe with the GT drivetrain/suspension/wheels.  Either way it comes out the same.  Seems like you could either one or the other.  The SS/RS Camaro wasn't one of those dealer involved overlay things.  It was a true production code/model.  I've got a buddy of mine with 2.  One has about 100,000 miles and is his daily driver (although just about perfect) and the other is just for show (less than 5,000 miles, 100% original down to the tires, and couldn't be any more perfect if you tried).||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||08-25-2000||10:07 AM||||Actually, I was thinking of making a Formula/GT. I have an '88 formula, but I love the 88 fast backs and the front end of the '86 gts. So, swap the front and tail, and I'd still have the stickers on the door saying formula. so technically, it could be a Formula w/ the fastback conversion.||||reg||1
